WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.537
Before the coding exercises, make sure you can explain the core ideas in your own words.

00:00:05.537 --> 00:00:12.652
First, why can a LEFT JOIN followed by a WHERE condition on the right table remove unmatched left rows?

00:00:12.652 --> 00:00:20.302
Your explanation should mention that unmatched right-side values become NULL and that WHERE filters the final

00:00:20.302 --> 00:00:24.667
joined result. Second, can you choose a join type from the population rule?

00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:27.757
Only matched records suggests INNER JOIN.

00:00:27.757 --> 00:00:32.992
Every patient suggests preserving patients with LEFT JOIN when patients are on the left.

00:00:32.992 --> 00:00:37.747
Reconciliation across both sources suggests FULL OUTER JOIN.

00:00:37.747 --> 00:00:43.912
Third, can you explain row multiplication without simply calling the extra rows duplicates?

00:00:43.912 --> 00:00:48.557
Several child tables with multiple matches can create combinations.

00:00:48.557 --> 00:00:53.622
The rows may be unique as combinations even though they inflate event counts.

00:00:53.622 --> 00:00:58.422
Finally, can you design a one-row-per-patient result safely?

00:00:58.422 --> 00:01:05.157
A strong answer is to summarize each child table to patient grain before joining it to the patient master.

00:01:05.157 --> 00:01:10.517
If you can explain these ideas clearly, the syntax becomes much easier.

00:01:10.517 --> 00:01:12.887
The goal is not memorization.

00:01:12.887 --> 00:01:16.642
The goal is reasoning about the data structure and the business question.
